    # Original mock response is preserved from the supplied app.py.
    # Replace this return with graph.invoke(initial_state) when ready for live analysis.
    # return {"code":"\"\"\"Security issue: hardcoded credential.\"\"\"\r\n\r\nAPI_TOKEN = \"sk_test_EXAMPLE_DO_NOT_USE_123456\"\r\n\r\n\r\ndef call_service(client):\r\n    return client.get(\"/profile\", headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {API_TOKEN}\"})\r\n","code_findings":[{"category":"Code Smell","description":"The module-level constant API_TOKEN is a hardcoded credential embedded directly in source code. This is a major anti-pattern: secrets should be supplied from the environment or a secret manager at runtime. If this file were checked into version control, the token would persist in git history, be visible to anyone with repository access, and be extremely difficult to rotate. Even though the value appears to be an example/test token, the pattern itself is dangerous and sets a bad precedent.","issue":"Hardcoded credential in source code","line_number":3,"severity":"CRITICAL"},{"category":"Design Issue","description":"call_service accepts a client and then injects the hardcoded API_TOKEN into the Authorization header itself, rather than relying on the client to carry its own authentication configuration. This mixes transport (the client) with credential management (the token), couples the function to a specific secret, makes it non-reusable across environments, and hides the dependency on API_TOKEN from the function signature. Callers cannot tell that this function depends on a global secret.","issue":"Authentication coupled to a global constant instead of the client","line_number":6,"severity":"MEDIUM"},{"category":"Documentation","description":"Neither the module nor call_service has a docstring explaining its purpose, expected client type, return value, or error behavior. The only docstring present is the note 'Security issue: hardcoded credential.', which documents a problem rather than the intended use of the code. This reduces maintainability and makes the code harder for others to understand and reuse correctly.","issue":"Missing functional documentation","line_number":1,"severity":"LOW"}],"language":"Python","messages":[],"pr_summary":{"key_issues":["CRITICAL: Hardcoded credential (API_TOKEN) on line 3 — a secret is embedded directly in source code, which would persist in version control history, be visible to anyone with repository access, and be difficult to rotate. The token must be removed, loaded from the environment or a secret manager at runtime, and rotated because it is considered exposed.","HIGH: The hardcoded API token is used as a Bearer credential in the Authorization header for the /profile request (line 3), allowing anyone with access to the source, logs, or compiled artifacts to impersonate the application and access protected data.","MEDIUM: call_service (line 6) couples authentication to a module-level global constant rather than to the client or an explicit parameter, mixing transport with credential management, reducing reusability across environments, and hiding the dependency on API_TOKEN from the function signature.","LOW: Missing functional documentation — neither the module nor call_service has a docstring describing purpose, parameters, return value, or error behavior; the only present docstring documents a security problem instead of intended use."],"merge_recommendation":"Blocked until the hardcoded credential is removed from source, loaded from the environment/secret manager at runtime, the exposed token is rotated, and the authentication coupling is addressed. The CRITICAL and HIGH findings represent a material security risk that cannot be merged in its current state; the MEDIUM and LOW items should also be resolved before merge.","overview":"The pull request introduces a call_service function that retrieves a /profile endpoint using a hardcoded API token (API_TOKEN) embedded as a module-level constant. The analysis identified a CRITICAL hardcoded-credential issue and a related HIGH-severity hardcoded secret usage in the Authorization header, both stemming from the same underlying defect on line 3. In addition, a MEDIUM design issue flags that authentication is coupled to a global constant rather than being injected into the client or the function signature, reducing reusability and testability. A LOW documentation issue notes the absence of functional docstrings for the module and function. Remediation guidance is provided for all findings, centered on externalizing secrets, rotating the exposed token, decoupling credential management from the function body, and adding proper documentation.","positive_observations":["Remediation recommendations are provided for all four findings, with concrete corrected-code examples and best-practice guidance (e.g., loading the token from os.environ, failing fast when the variable is unset, passing the token as an explicit parameter, and adding proper docstrings)."],"recommendations":["(CRITICAL, line 3) Remove the hardcoded API_TOKEN value from source. Load the credential at runtime from an environment variable (e.g., os.environ[\"MY_SERVICE_API_TOKEN\"]) or a secret manager (AWS Secrets Manager, HashiCorp Vault, GCP Secret Manager). Add the variable name to a .env.example without the value, ensure the real value is never committed, and fail fast with a clear error if the variable is missing at startup.","(HIGH, line 3) Treat the exposed token as compromised: revoke/rotate it immediately and verify no service is still using the old value. Do not log the token or the Authorization header. Resolve the token from the environment/secret store rather than a code literal.","(MEDIUM, line 6) Decouple authentication from the function body: either pass the token (or token provider) as an explicit parameter to call_service, or preconfigure the client with its auth credentials at construction time so call_service simply invokes client.get('/profile') with no header injection. Avoid reading a module-level global inside the function.","(LOW, line 1) Replace the 'Security issue: hardcoded credential.' note with proper functional documentation: a module docstring describing purpose, and a call_service docstring covering purpose, parameters (including expected client type), return value, and error behavior. Track the security defect in an issue tracker rather than in code comments."],"review_status":"CHANGES_REQUESTED","severity_breakdown":{"critical":1,"high":1,"informational":0,"low":1,"medium":1},"title":"PR Review: Hardcoded API Credential and Authentication Coupling in call_service","total_findings":4},"remediation_findings":[{"best_practice":"Never embed secrets in source code. Secrets must be supplied at runtime from a secure secret store or environment variable, kept out of version control, and rotated regularly. Centralizing secret retrieval in one place makes auditing and rotation easier.","corrected_code":"import os\n\n# Load the credential from the environment (or a secret manager) at runtime.\n# The value must never be committed to version control.\nAPI_TOKEN = os.environ[\"MY_SERVICE_API_TOKEN\"]  # raises KeyError if unset\n\ndef call_service(client):\n    return client.get(\"/profile\", headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {API_TOKEN}\"})","fix_recommendation":"Remove the hardcoded token value from the source. Load it at runtime from an environment variable injected by the deployment platform, or from a secret manager (e.g., AWS Secrets Manager, HashiCorp Vault, GCP Secret Manager). Add the variable name to a .env.example (without the value) and ensure the real value is never committed to version control. Because the token was exposed in source, rotate/revoke it and issue a new one. If the variable is missing at startup, fail fast with a clear error rather than falling back to a default.","issue":"Hardcoded credential in source code","line_number":3,"severity":"CRITICAL"},{"best_practice":"Keep secrets out of code and out of anything that can be inspected after deployment (source, logs, artifacts). Retrieve credentials at runtime from a secure store, and treat any token that has been exposed as compromised and subject to rotation.","corrected_code":"import os\n\n# Fetch the Bearer token from the environment at runtime.\nAPI_TOKEN = os.environ[\"MY_SERVICE_API_TOKEN\"]\n\ndef call_service(client):\n    # The Authorization header is built from the runtime-supplied secret,\n    # not from a literal value in the code.\n    return client.get(\"/profile\", headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {API_TOKEN}\"})","fix_recommendation":"Stop referencing a module-level credential literal. Resolve the token from the environment/secret manager (see the CRITICAL finding) so the value is not present in the Authorization header construction path as a hardcoded string. Do not log the token or the Authorization header. Rotate the exposed token immediately and verify no service is still using the old value.","issue":"Hardcoded API token credential","line_number":3,"severity":"HIGH"},{"best_practice":"Functions should declare their dependencies explicitly. Credential management is best handled by the client (or an injected token provider), not by a hidden global. This makes the function testable, reusable across environments, and its dependencies visible in the signature.","corrected_code":"def call_service(client, api_token: str):\n    \"\"\"Fetch the caller's profile using the provided client and token.\n\n    The client is responsible for transport; the token is supplied by the\n    caller so this function has no hidden dependency on a global secret.\n    \"\"\"\n    return client.get(\"/profile\", headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {api_token}\"})\n\n# Usage example:\n# import os\n# token = os.environ[\"MY_SERVICE_API_TOKEN\"]\n# call_service(client, token)","fix_recommendation":"Decouple authentication from the function body. Either (a) pass the token (or a token provider) as an explicit parameter to call_service so the dependency is visible in the signature, or (b) configure the client with its auth credentials at construction time and have call_service simply invoke client.get('/profile') with no header injection. Option (b) is cleaner if the client library supports auth configuration. Avoid reading a module-level global inside the function.","issue":"Authentication coupled to a global constant instead of the client","line_number":6,"severity":"MEDIUM"},{"best_practice":"Docstrings should document intended behavior (purpose, parameters, return value, exceptions), not known defects. Problems belong in issue trackers, not in code comments.","corrected_code":"\"\"\"Service client helpers for the profile API.\"\"\"\n\ndef call_service(client, api_token: str):\n    \"\"\"Retrieve the authenticated user's profile.\n\n    Args:\n        client: An HTTP client preconfigured for the target service.\n        api_token: A Bearer token with access to the /profile endpoint.\n\n    Returns:\n        The response object returned by the client for the /profile request.\n\n    Raises:\n        HTTPError: If the underlying request fails with a non-2xx status.\n    \"\"\"\n    return client.get(\"/profile\", headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {api_token}\"})","fix_recommendation":"Replace the 'Security issue: hardcoded credential.' note with proper functional documentation. Add a module docstring describing the purpose of the module, and a docstring on call_service covering its purpose, parameters (including expected client type), return value, and error behavior. Track the security defect in an issue tracker rather than in a code comment.","issue":"Missing functional documentation","line_number":1,"severity":"LOW"}],"security_findings":[{"category":"Hardcoded Secret","description":"An API token is hardcoded as a module-level constant and then used as a Bearer credential in the Authorization header for the /profile request. Any party with access to the source code, repository, logs, or compiled artifact can obtain the credential and impersonate the application to access protected data. This violates the principle of keeping secrets out of code. Remediation: load the token from a secure secret store or environment variable at runtime, avoid committing it to version control, and rotate the exposed token.","issue":"Hardcoded API token credential","line_number":3,"severity":"HIGH"}],"unified_findings":[{"category":"Code Smell","description":"The module-level constant API_TOKEN is a hardcoded credential embedded directly in source code. This is a major anti-pattern: secrets should be supplied from the environment or a secret manager at runtime. If this file were checked into version control, the token would persist in git history, be visible to anyone with repository access, and be extremely difficult to rotate. Even though the value appears to be an example/test token, the pattern itself is dangerous and sets a bad precedent.","issue":"Hardcoded credential in source code","line_number":3,"severity":"CRITICAL"},{"category":"Hardcoded Secret","description":"An API token is hardcoded as a module-level constant and then used as a Bearer credential in the Authorization header for the /profile request. Any party with access to the source code, repository, logs, or compiled artifact can obtain the credential and impersonate the application to access protected data. This violates the principle of keeping secrets out of code. Remediation: load the token from a secure secret store or environment variable at runtime, avoid committing it to version control, and rotate the exposed token.","issue":"Hardcoded API token credential","line_number":3,"severity":"HIGH"},{"category":"Design Issue","description":"call_service accepts a client and then injects the hardcoded API_TOKEN into the Authorization header itself, rather than relying on the client to carry its own authentication configuration. This mixes transport (the client) with credential management (the token), couples the function to a specific secret, makes it non-reusable across environments, and hides the dependency on API_TOKEN from the function signature. Callers cannot tell that this function depends on a global secret.","issue":"Authentication coupled to a global constant instead of the client","line_number":6,"severity":"MEDIUM"},{"category":"Documentation","description":"Neither the module nor call_service has a docstring explaining its purpose, expected client type, return value, or error behavior. The only docstring present is the note 'Security issue: hardcoded credential.', which documents a problem rather than the intended use of the code. This reduces maintainability and makes the code harder for others to understand and reuse correctly.","issue":"Missing functional documentation","line_number":1,"severity":"LOW"}]}
    return graph.invoke(initial_state)